INT. OPERA HOUSE, FLY GALLERY - NIGHT

Far above the stage, a catwalk of iron grating hung with ropes and counterweights. Below, the orchestra tunes in a distant tide. Four figures in stagehand blacks creep along the rail.

The first is IMOGEN LARKSPUR-ADEWALE (40s), who holds a lantern shuttered to a slit. The second is HOLDEN PRYCE-NWANKWO (50s), who carries a coil of rope over one shoulder. The third is SALOME BRAITHWAITE-OKORO (30s), who moves like water. The fourth, last in line, is YEVGENY "ZHENYA" ABARA-KOZLOV (20s), who is sweating through his gloves.

She raises two fingers. He freezes behind her. She points at a hanging chandelier of painted canvas, ten feet away. He nods, though he cannot see what she means. She takes the rope from him, loops it round the rail, and tests it with her weight. He swallows. The chandelier turns slowly in the draft.

IMOGEN
(whispering)
Ten minutes.

HOLDEN
Eight.

SALOME
Seven, if he stops shaking.

ZHENYA
I'm not shaking.

SALOME
You're humming.

ZHENYA
I'm humming because he's humming.

HOLDEN
Nobody's humming.

They listen. Below, the orchestra falls silent. A hush rolls up through the dark. A single violin begins, and from beneath the stage a soprano's voice rises in a glissando so pure it makes Imogen's grip tighten on the lantern.

She turns to him, and he to her. Her eyes glitter. His mouth is open. Whoever they are to each other, in this moment they share a single trembling breath.

SALOME
(pointing)
Now.

They move. She drops the rope. He catches it. She slides down. He braces. The chandelier lurches, and a hundred glass beads shiver. Below, someone in the audience gasps.

IMOGEN
(hissing)
Abort!

HOLDEN
Too late!

The chandelier swings free and crashes silently into the curtain, a harmless explosion of painted canvas. The audience, delighted, applauds. The four of them crouch on the catwalk, frozen, as the soprano holds her note and the whole house roars.
